I'm doing a read-only privilege review of this Postgres instance and want to know who can do what before I touch any settings. Please don't create, modify, or delete anything — every step is a read.

Start by mapping the schemas on the instance so I know what's in scope. Then enumerate the objects inside information_schema so I can confirm which role/grant views are available, and pull column metadata for information_schema.role_table_grants and pg_catalog.pg_roles so my later SELECTs reference the real column names.

Once that's grounded, give me five self-describing queries, each prefixed with a short literal label row so the output reads cleanly on its own:

(1) a role count from pg_roles, cast to text;
(2) a bounded sample of role_table_grants scoped to objects in information_schema — columns grantee, table_schema, table_name, privilege_type, is_grantable, all cast to text, ordered by grantee, table_name and limited to the top 10 rows;
(3) the distinct count of grantees appearing in role_table_grants on objects in information_schema, cast to text;
(4) an identity pair showing current_user and session_user, each cast to text;
(5) a bounded oid-ordered snapshot of pg_roles showing oid, rolname, rolsuper cast to text, rolcanlogin cast to text, and rolcreatedb cast to text — rolpassword must be excluded — ordered by oid and limited to 10 rows.

Finish with a connection-scope database health check so I can see whether the current connection setup is already flagged for anything. No DDL, no DML, and no reads against any user table — system catalogs only.

